PKDCLX 1.1.2 - vendored for ZIP method 10 (DCL Implode)
=======================================================

Source
------
Downloaded from the Xtro-Zip tree (johnsonjh/infozip-av, branch pkav),
used as a behavioural reference for method 10 while comparing our ZIP
support against unzip60:

    unzip60/pkdcl.c
    unzip60/pkdcl.h

License (from the source header):
    PKDCLX 1.1.2
    Copyright (c) 2026 Jeffrey H. Johnson <johnsonjh.dev@gmail.com>
    SPDX-License-Identifier: MIT-0

MIT-0 is compatible with the zlib/libbzip2 licensing already used by the
bundled decompression libraries, so vendoring is acceptable.

Why this instead of blast.c
---------------------------
PKWARE's original `blast.c` decoder was vendored first, but it keeps its
Huffman decode tables in file-scope statics (`litcnt`, `litsym`, ...),
which makes it neither re-entrant nor safe to call repeatedly in a
long-lived DLL process. pkdcl keeps all decoder state in a stack-local
`struct dec_state`, so repeated and concurrent calls are safe.

pkdcl also ships a real DCL *encoder* (`pkdcl_implode` /
`pkdcl_implode_ex`), which is what our method-10 test fixtures are
generated with. The earlier hand-rolled Python DCL encoder produced
streams that blast.c happily decoded but that were not spec-conformant;
pkdcl rejected them, proving the fixtures were wrong rather than the
decoder.

Interface notes
---------------
* `pkdcl_read_func` returns the number of BYTES READ, not a boolean.
  Returning a nonzero "success" value smaller than the count silently
  truncates the stream.
* The legacy `pkdcl_explode()` rejects a first fill of <= 4 bytes, so an
  empty member (which is exactly 4 bytes) must use
  `pkdcl_explode_ex()` instead. `CZipTool::UnDcl` does this.
* `PKDCL_EXPLODE_WORK_SIZE` (12574) is the caller-supplied work buffer.
  `CZipTool::UnDcl` heap-allocates it per member.
* The ZIP payload for method 10 is the raw DCL stream, including its
  3-byte prologue (ctype, dictionary-size bits, initial bit buffer).
  There is no ZIP-level framing around it.
* `PKDCL_ENABLE_LEGACY_API` is NOT defined, so the `implode`/`explode`/
  `crc32` legacy symbols stay out of the DLL namespace and cannot
  collide with zlib's `crc32`.

Only `pkdcl.c` and `pkdcl.h` are vendored. The encoder, the file I/O
helpers (`xm_read`, `xb_*`, `x_write_all`) and the command-line driver
inside pkdcl.c are compiled but unreferenced; they are dead-stripped by
the linker.